<article class="env-lemma" id="064Z">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/064Z">Lemma <span data-tag="064Z">15.66.11</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a ring map. Assume that $B$ is pseudo-coherent as an $A$-module. Let $K^\bullet $ be a complex of $B$-modules. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$K^\bullet $ is $m$-pseudo-coherent as a complex of $B$-modules, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$K^\bullet $ is $m$-pseudo-coherent as a complex of $A$-modules. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> The same equivalence holds for pseudo-coherence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume (1). Choose a bounded complex of finite free $B$-modules $E^\bullet $ and a map $\alpha : E^\bullet \to K^\bullet $ which is an isomorphism on cohomology in degrees $> m$ and a surjection in degree $m$. Consider the distinguished triangle $(E^\bullet , K^\bullet , C(\alpha )^\bullet )$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/064W" data-tag="064W">15.66.7</a> $C(\alpha )^\bullet $ is $m$-pseudo-coherent as a complex of $A$-modules. Hence it suffices to prove that $E^\bullet $ is pseudo-coherent as a complex of $A$-modules, which follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/064Y" data-tag="064Y">15.66.9</a>. The pseudo-coherent case of (1) $\Rightarrow $ (2) follows from this and Lemma <a href="/tag/064U" data-tag="064U">15.66.5</a>. 
    <p>
      Assume (2). Let $n$ be the largest integer such that $H^ n(K^\bullet ) \not= 0$. We will prove that $K^\bullet $ is $m$-pseudo-coherent as a complex of $B$-modules by induction on $n - m$. The case $n < m$ follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/064W" data-tag="064W">15.66.7</a>. Choose a bounded complex of finite free $A$-modules $E^\bullet $ and a map $\alpha : E^\bullet \to K^\bullet $ which is an isomorphism on cohomology in degrees $> m$ and a surjection in degree $m$. Consider the induced map of complexes 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \alpha \otimes 1 : E^\bullet \otimes _ A B \to K^\bullet .  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Note that $C(\alpha \otimes 1)^\bullet $ is acyclic in degrees $\geq n$ as $H^ n(E) \to H^ n(E^\bullet \otimes _ A B) \to H^ n(K^\bullet )$ is surjective by construction and since $H^ i(E^\bullet \otimes _ A B) = 0$ for $i > n$ by the spectral sequence of Example <a href="/tag/0662" data-tag="0662">15.63.4</a>. On the other hand, $C(\alpha \otimes 1)^\bullet $ is $m$-pseudo-coherent as a complex of $A$-modules because both $K^\bullet $ and $E^\bullet \otimes _ A B$ (see Lemma <a href="/tag/064Y" data-tag="064Y">15.66.9</a>) are so, see Lemma <a href="/tag/064R" data-tag="064R">15.66.2</a>. Hence by induction we see that $C(\alpha \otimes 1)^\bullet $ is $m$-pseudo-coherent as a complex of $B$-modules. Finally another application of Lemma <a href="/tag/064R" data-tag="064R">15.66.2</a> shows that $K^\bullet $ is $m$-pseudo-coherent as a complex of $B$-modules (as clearly $E^\bullet \otimes _ A B$ is pseudo-coherent as a complex of $B$-modules). The pseudo-coherent case of (2) $\Rightarrow $ (1) follows from this and Lemma <a href="/tag/064U" data-tag="064U">15.66.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>